  "assessment_summary": "This paper examines the relationship between public science (knowledge, human capital, and inventions) and corporate R&D. It uses an instrumental variable approach, exploiting firm-specific exposure to changes in federal agency R&D budgets driven by the political composition of congressional appropriations subcommittees, to address endogeneity concerns. The study finds that: (1) abstract public knowledge (publications) has little effect on corporate R&D; (2) public inventions (university patents) reduce corporate R&D; and (3) human capital (PhDs) positively affects corporate R&D. These effects vary across firms and industries, with firms on the technology frontier responding differently than followers. The paper contributes to the literature by focusing on established firms and differentiating the impact of different components of public science on corporate R&D. A key strength is the rigorous econometric approach using instrumental variables to address endogeneity. The use of textual similarity measures and bibliometric linkages to connect federal funding to publications, dissertations, and patents is also novel and valuable. However, some limitations exist: First, the reliance on publicly traded firms may limit the generalizability of the findings to smaller or private firms. Second, while the instrumental variable approach helps address endogeneity, the identifying assumptions may still be debated. The assumption that federal funding is driven by political factors and not technological ones may be difficult to fully validate. Third, the use of historical data (1980-2015) may limit the relevance of the findings to current innovation dynamics, given the increasing role of startups and open innovation models. Fourth, the study does not fully explore the mechanisms through which public inventions compete with corporate R&D, or why firms have limited ability to absorb abstract ideas. Fifth, while the paper considers heterogeneity across firms and industries, more nuanced analyses of specific technology sectors or types of innovation could provide additional insights. Finally, although several robustness checks are performed, there may be additional unobserved factors that could influence the results. These threats suggest some caution in interpreting the magnitude and precise estimates, even if the main qualitative results appear relatively robust. The summary statistics presented in Appendix Table C16 show substantial differences between industries, suggesting that a single national-level analysis could obscure important heterogeneity and potential omitted variable bias. It is also unclear whether or not the methods are likely to generalize to international contexts, which would represent a substantial strengthening of the paper.",
